content:
{
  "summary": "This scan found four items, all about the user accounts on this computer. The most important is that the Guest account is turned on without needing a password, which should be looked at first. The built-in Administrator account is also turned on, and there are two extra administrator accounts you should simply confirm you recognize. No other problems were reported.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Guest account is on and needs no password",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-GUEST-ENABLED:6684b413"
      ],
      "explanation": "The Guest account is enabled and does not require a password. That means anyone with access to this computer could sign in without having an account of their own. Most personal computers don't need Guest turned on. See the fix shown for this finding in the report."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Built-in Administrator account is on",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-BUILTIN-ADMIN-ENABLED:1b4c5dc7"
      ],
      "explanation": "Windows has a built-in account called Administrator that is normally kept off. On this computer it is turned on. It does require a password, which helps, but because every attacker knows this account name exists, it is a common target for password guessing. Unless you deliberately use it, consider the fix shown in the report."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "Two extra administrator accounts to confirm",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291",
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:3df7405c"
      ],
      "explanation": "Two local accounts, <computer>\\<account-2> and <computer>\\<account-3>, have full administrator rights. This is often perfectly normal, for example your own account or a family member's. Just check that you recognize both. If you don't recognize one, treat that seriously, since an unknown administrator account can be a sign someone else got in. This scan wasn't compared with an earlier one, so it can't tell whether these accounts are new."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
